I reviewed `main...review-head`, which adds `average()` in `/home/jack/.t3/bench-runs/toy/clone-C2/pricing.py`. I found three real issues. The conventions angle had nothing to check because there's no CLAUDE.md at the user level or in the repo. Nothing calls `average()` yet, so there were no call sites to trace. Nothing was deleted, so no removed behavior to audit. I found no efficiency or altitude issues. I'm reporting three, under the target of five, because I didn't want to pad the list.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "pricing.py",
    "line": 17,
    "summary": "average() divides by len(prices) with no empty-list guard, so it raises ZeroDivisionError on an empty cart, which the PR body and docstring both say is a common case.",
    "failure_scenario": "A new cart has no items, so the summary panel calls average([]); total([]) returns 0, then 0 / 0 raises ZeroDivisionError and the panel fails to render instead of showing 0.0 or 'no items'."
  },
  {
    "file": "pricing.py",
    "line": 15,
    "summary": "The docstring says empty lists are common and implies they're handled, but the code doesn't handle them and the function doesn't say what it returns for an empty cart.",
    "failure_scenario": "A caller reads 'empty lists are common for new carts', assumes average([]) is safe, and skips its own guard; the summary panel then crashes on every new cart."
  },
  {
    "file": "test_pricing.py",
    "line": 1,
    "summary": "No tests were added for average(), neither for the normal case nor for the empty-cart case the PR says it supports.",
    "failure_scenario": "A test like average([]) would have caught the ZeroDivisionError before merge; without one, the empty-cart crash only shows up in the summary panel in production."
  }
]
